   "source": [
    "# Research Question"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To what extent can pre-release structural characteristics—such as platform, genre, publisher, and release year—predict a video game’s relative commercial success, measured by its percentile rank of global sales within its release year and classified into four bestseller levels (Top 10%, 75–90%, 25–75%, Bottom 25%)?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Background and Prior Work"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Instructions: REPLACE the contents of this cell with your work, including any updates to recover points lost in your proposal feedback"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Hypothesis\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Using data related to video game sales across different platforms, genres, publishers, and release years, the final model that is planned to create should be able to predict year-normalized sales scores, the relative commercial success (“Bestseller Level”) of a video game, using a dataset from around 1990 to 2016. \n",
    "\n",
    "In the process, we plan to use multinomial logistic regression as a  baseline, random forest as the main model to predict bestseller Level using pre-release features. After data wrangling, we will establish the object variable, create the baseline model, the random forest, and last but not least, the data comparison. \n",
    "\n",
    "When the model is complete, we will use research information outside of the dataset that is after 2016 to test the accuracy of the model. Currently, we believe that the model will be able to predict the year-normalized sales scores at a pretty good level. The accuracy would be around 80% using a normalized percentage scale. \n",
    "\n",
    "Also, we predict that while testing, we will find RPG games to have the highest rate of having high year-normalized sales scores. To testify, we will compare the model hypothesis and actual sales scores of RPG games with others. \n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Data overview\n",
    "\n",
    "There are five primary variables used in our dataset: platform, genre, publisher, release year, and global sales.\n",
    "\n",
    "Platform indicates the distribution platform on which a game was released, such as Steam or PlayStation.\n",
    "\n",
    "Genre refers to the categorical classification of each game. We adopt the genre categories provided by the dataset, which align with commonly used industry classifications, such as MOBA and platformer.\n",
    "\n",
    "Release year ranges from 1980 to 2016. Each year is treated as a distinct cohort in our analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "Global sales represent the total lifetime worldwide sales of each game, measured in millions of copies sold.\n",
    "\n",
    "To measure a game’s relative commercial success, we compare each game’s total lifetime global sales against those of other games released in the same year and determine its percentile ranking within that release-year cohort.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, a game released in 1981 is compared only with other games released in 1981, regardless of how long those games remained on the market. This cohort-based percentile approach allows us to evaluate relative performance while controlling for differences in overall market size across years.\n",
    "\n",
    "Based on the within-year percentile, we classify games into four bestseller levels:\n",
    "\n",
    "Gold — Top 10%\n",
    "\n",
    "Silver — 75th–90th percentile\n",
    "\n",
    "Breakout — 25th–75th percentile\n",
    "\n",
    "Indie Survival — Bottom 25%\n",
    "\n",
    "There are several limitations to this strategy. First, the dataset does not include revenue data; only the number of copies sold is available. Because price variation across games is unobserved, this sales-based measure may introduce bias toward lower-priced games that achieve higher unit sales. Consequently, the “success” defined in this study reflects relative popularity in terms of market penetration rather than total economic profitability.\n",
    "\n",
    "Despite these limitations, the percentile-based classification provides a consistent and interpretable measure of commercial performance relative to contemporaneous market conditions within each release year.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "As discussed in the Background section, our dataset includes only structured pre-release characteristics and does not incorporate dynamic factors such as marketing exposure, demo quality, developer reputation, or early user reviews. These promotional and post-release elements can have a substantial impact on sales performance, particularly on platforms such as Steam.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because post-release factors are inherently unavailable prior to launch, they are excluded from our predictive model. Although certain pre-release promotional information may exist before launch, it is not consistently recorded or standardized within our dataset and therefore cannot be reliably incorporated.\n",
    "\n",
    "Consequently, our model may omit influential determinants of commercial success, which could limit its overall predictive accuracy.\n"
   ]
  },
